  "name": "Question 15",
  "problem": "Express $z=2(\\operatorname{cos}\u2061(\\frac{5\\pi }{3})+\\text{i}\\operatorname{sin}\u2061(\\frac{5\\pi }{3}))$ in the form $z=x+\\text{i}y.$",
  "worked_solution": "To express the number in Cartesian form, we expand the parentheses and substitute the values of the cosine and sine.\n\nFirst, we find $\\operatorname{cos}\u2061(\\frac{5\\pi }{3})$ and $\\operatorname{sin}\u2061(\\frac{5\\pi }{3}):$\n \n\n$$\n\\operatorname{cos}\u2061(\\frac{5\\pi }{3})=\\operatorname{cos}\u2061(-\\frac{\\pi }{3})=\\operatorname{cos}\u2061(\\frac{\\pi }{3})=\\frac{1}{2},\n$$\n\n \n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sin}\u2061(\\frac{5\\pi }{3})=\\operatorname{sin}\u2061(-\\frac{\\pi }{3})=-\\operatorname{sin}\u2061(\\frac{\\pi }{3})=-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}.\n$$\n\nNow, we substitute the values and expand the parentheses:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}z & =2(\\operatorname{cos}\u2061(\\frac{5\\pi }{3})+\\text{i}\\operatorname{sin}\u2061(\\frac{5\\pi }{3})) \\\\  & =2(\\frac{1}{2}-\\text{i}\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}) \\\\  & =1-\\sqrt{3}\\,\\text{i}\\end{aligned}\n$$",
